.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pki
manages the certificate authorities that
.BR pkid (8)
runs on this machine.
It asks
.B pkid
to create, sign and change CAs, and
.B pkid
decides whether you can, by your own access token: what
.B pki
can do is exactly what you can.
.PP
A CA's key stays with
.BR pkid .
In
.BR pkid 's
own store it is readable by
.B pkid
alone, which signs with it unattended.
A key kept in a sealed file instead, such as a root's on removable media, is sealed under a passphrase and never kept open:
.B pki
asks for the passphrase each time the key signs.
.PP
Name a CA by its ID or by its name.
Names compare without regard to case; when two CAs share a name, use the ID.
Name a template the same way, by its GUID or its name.
.PP
Templates and names are not
.BR pkid 's
to change but an administrator's:
.B pki
writes them to the Registry under
.BR Machine\eSoftware\ePki ,
and
.B pkid
takes each change up as soon as it is written.
.B pki
checks a change by the rules
.B pkid
loads it with before writing it, since
.B pkid
issues nothing while any of its configuration is invalid.
.PP
.B pki
reads certificates and requests in PEM or DER, and writes them in PEM.
It never overwrites a file: an output file must not exist already.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Prepare this machine for
.BR pkid :
create its state directories under
.IR /var/state/pki ,
and seed its configuration in the Registry under
.BR Machine\eSoftware\ePki :
the Server and Device templates if there are no templates, the roots of the name trees, and the key where
.B pkid
records the CAs created through it.
.B setup
only adds what is missing.
It never replaces a value an administrator has set, so it is safe to run again.
Only an administrator can run it.
.TP
.B ca list
List the CAs: name, ID, and whether each issues or awaits its certificate.
.TP
.BI "ca show " ca
Show a CA in full: its key, the templates it issues from, and its certificate's subject, issuer, serial number, validity and SHA-256 fingerprint.
With
.BI \-\-out " file" ,
also write its certificate to
.IR file ,
such as to give to the machines that are to trust it; a CA awaiting its certificate writes the request instead.
.TP
.BI "ca create " name
Create a CA with a new key.
Give one of:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-root
A root CA, which signs its own certificate.
.TP
.BI \-\-issuer " ca"
A subordinate of another CA on this
.BR pkid ,
which signs its certificate now.
.TP
.BI \-\-offline " request-file"
A subordinate of a CA elsewhere.
.B pki
writes the request for that CA to sign to
.IR request-file ;
the new CA issues nothing until its certificate is installed with
.BR "ca install" .
.RE
.TP
.BI "ca sign " "ca request-file"
Sign another CA's request with
.IR ca 's
key, making it a subordinate of
.IR ca ,
for a CA that runs elsewhere.
.B \-\-name
names the subordinate CA.
The certificate goes to standard output, or to the file
.B \-\-out
names.
.TP
.BI "ca install " "ca certificate-file"
Install the certificate a CA created with
.B \-\-offline
awaits.
Its key must be the CA's own.
.TP
.BI "ca import " "name certificate-file sealed-key-file"
Take up an existing CA, such as one restored from a backup, from its certificate and its key as
.B ca export\-key
wrote it.
.B pki
asks for the passphrase the key file is sealed under.
.TP
.BI "ca export\-key " "ca file"
Write
.IR ca 's
key to
.IR file ,
sealed under a new passphrase, such as for a backup.
A key in
.BR pkid 's
store can be exported only if it was created or imported with
.BR \-\-exportable .
The file is readable by its owner alone.
.TP
.BI "ca set " ca
Change which templates
.I ca
issues from, or the CRL distribution points its certificates name.
Each list given replaces the current one; a list not given stays as it is.
.TP
.BI "request submit " "ca template request-file"
Ask
.I ca
for a certificate, under one of the templates it offers.
.I ca
issues it at once, holds it for an officer to approve, or refuses it, as its template and the name trees decide.
An issued certificate goes to standard output, or to the file
.B \-\-out
names.
A held request's number is printed, to collect the certificate by once it is approved.
.TP
.BI "request list " ca
List
.IR ca 's
requests, oldest first: number, status, template, when it was submitted, and the names it asked for.
.BI \-\-status " status"
lists only those that are
.BR pending ,
.B issued
or
.BR denied .
.TP
.BI "request show " "ca request"
Show a request in full, including who submitted it, who decided it and why it was held or refused.
.TP
.BI "request approve " "ca request"
Approve a held request, issuing its certificate.
Only an officer of the CA can, and never for a request they submitted themselves.
.TP
.BI "request deny " "ca request"
Refuse a held request.
.BI \-\-reason " text"
records why.
.TP
.BI "cert list " ca
List the certificates
.I ca
has issued, in order of their requests: serial number, request, whether each is valid, expired or revoked, when it expires, and its subject.
.B \-\-unrevoked
leaves out revoked certificates.
.TP
.BI "cert show " "ca serial"
Show a certificate, with its revocation if it has one.
.B \-\-out
also writes it to a file.
.TP
.BI "cert get " "ca request"
Fetch the certificate issued for a request, such as one an officer has since approved, to standard output or the file
.B \-\-out
names.
.TP
.BI "cert revoke " "ca serial"
Revoke a certificate.
.BI \-\-reason " reason"
gives the reason, as RFC 5280 names it:
.BR unspecified ,
the default,
.BR key\-compromise ,
.BR ca\-compromise ,
.BR affiliation\-changed ,
.BR superseded ,
.B cessation\-of\-operation
or
.BR privilege\-withdrawn .
Revocation cannot be undone.
.TP
.BI "enroll " "ca template"
Make a key on this machine, ask
.I ca
to certify it under
.I template
for the names given, and write the key with its certificate and the CA certificates above it that you have a right at to the PKCS #12 file
.B \-\-out
names, such as to import into a phone for EAP-TLS.
Give at least one name, with
.BR \-\-dns ,
.B \-\-email
or
.BR \-\-upn ;
each can be repeated.
The key never reaches
.BR pkid ,
which sees only the request.
.B pki
asks for a new passphrase to protect the file.
.IP
If the request is held for approval,
.B pki
seals the key under the same passphrase in a file beside the one it would have written, named with
.B .key
added, and prints the command that collects the certificate once it is approved:
.B \-\-request
and
.B \-\-key
name the request and that file, and
.B pki
writes the PKCS #12 file then.
.IP
.BI \-\-algorithm " algorithm"
chooses the key, as for
.BR "ca create" ;
the default is
.BR ecdsa\-p256 .
.BI \-\-friendly\-name " text"
labels the key in the file; the default is the first name.
.B \-\-passphrase\-file
gives the passphrase as a file's first line.
.IP
The file uses the encryption OpenSSL 3 writes by default, AES-256 with PBKDF2 and SHA-256.
Current versions of OpenSSL, Windows and Firefox import it; Apple systems older than iOS 18 and macOS 15 are reported to refuse it.
.TP
.B template list
List the templates: name, GUID, whether they issue at once or hold for approval, lifetime, and purposes.
.TP
.BI "template show " template
Show a template in full, with its enrollment descriptor in SDDL and the CAs that issue from it.
.TP
.BI "template create " name
Create a template.
.BI \-\-from " template"
starts from a copy of another; without it, give at least
.BR \-\-purpose ,
.B \-\-name\-type
and
.BR \-\-lifetime ,
and the rest are the shipped templates' choices, except that renewal is due only at expiry unless
.B \-\-renewal\-window
is given.
A new template is offered by no CA until
.B ca set
adds it.
.TP
.BI "template set " template
Change a template.
Options not given leave their part of it as it is.
.TP
.BI "template delete " template
Delete a template.
A template a CA still issues from cannot be deleted.
.TP
.B name list
List the names that have settings of their own: their owner, and whether their descriptor is their own or inherited.
.TP
.BI "name show " name
Show who can request
.I name
\(em its request descriptor, in SDDL, and where it comes from \(em and who owns it.
.TP
.BI "name add " name
Add
.I name
to its tree, optionally with an owner.
.TP
.BI "name remove " name
Remove
.IR name .
A name with names below it, and a tree's root, cannot be removed.
.TP
.BI "name set\-security " "name sddl"
Set who can request
.I name
and, through entries marked to inherit (CI), the names below it.
The rights are Request (0x1), which issues without approval, Request with approval (0x2), which holds the request for an officer, and Wildcard (0x4), which also permits
.BI *. name .
PRINCIPAL SELF (PS) matches the name's owner.
.B pki
adds the entries
.I name
inherits from above, so that grants made higher up, such as Administrators', still apply.
.B \-\-inherit
removes the name's own descriptor instead, so that it inherits again.
.TP
.BI "name set\-owner " "name sid"
Make the principal
.I sid
the owner of exactly
.IR name ;
.B \-\-none
leaves it without one.
Certificates for names a principal owns carry its SID.
.TP
.BR help " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Print help for
.B pki
or one of its commands, as
.I command
.B \-\-help
does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print output as JSON; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SS Creating and importing CAs
These apply to
.B ca create
and
.BR "ca import" .
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " path"
Keep the CA's key in a file at
.IR path ,
sealed under a passphrase
.B pki
asks for, instead of in
.BR pkid 's
store.
.B pkid
writes the file, so
.I path
must be one it can write.
.TP
.B \-\-exportable
Let a key in
.BR pkid 's
store be exported with
.BR "ca export\-key" .
A key in the store that is not exportable can never leave
.BR pkid .
A key in a sealed file is already an export, and can always be exported.
.TP
.BI \-\-template " template"
Issue from
.IR template .
Repeat for more.
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " url"
Name
.I url
as a CRL distribution point in the certificates the CA issues.
Repeat for more.
.TP
.BI \-\-algorithm " algorithm"
For
.BR "ca create" :
the new key's algorithm, one of
.BR ecdsa\-p256 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p384 ,
.BR rsa\-2048 ,
.B rsa\-3072
and
.BR rsa\-4096 .
The default is
.B rsa\-4096
for a root, which any software can verify for as long as a root lasts, and
.B ecdsa\-p384
for any other CA.
.TP
.BI \-\-lifetime " days"
For
.B ca create
and
.BR "ca sign" :
how long the certificate is valid.
The default is 3650 days for a root and 1825 days for any other CA.
.TP
.BI \-\-path\-length " count"
For
.B ca create
and
.BR "ca sign" :
how many CAs can sit below the new one.
With none given there is no limit; 0 lets it issue only to people and machines.
.SS Changing a CA
These apply to
.BR "ca set" .
.TP
.BI \-\-template " template"
Issue from these templates only.
Repeat for more.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-templates
Issue from no templates, so that the CA takes no requests.
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " url"
Name these CRL distribution points only.
Repeat for more.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-crls
Name no CRL distribution points.
.SS Templates
These apply to
.B template create
and
.BR "template set" .
Each list option can be repeated, and the values given replace the template's list.
.TP
.BI \-\-purpose " purpose"
An extended key usage certificates carry:
.BR server\-auth ,
.BR client\-auth ,
.BR code\-signing ,
.BR email\-protection ,
.BR time\-stamping ,
.BR ocsp\-signing ,
or a dotted object identifier.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-usage " usage"
A key usage certificates carry:
.BR digital\-signature ,
.BR content\-commitment ,
.BR key\-encipherment ,
.B data\-encipherment
or
.BR key\-agreement .
.TP
.BI \-\-lifetime " days"
How long certificates last, from 1 to 7305 days.
.TP
.BI \-\-renewal\-window " days"
How many days before expiry renewal is due, fewer than the lifetime.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-algorithm " algorithm"
A kind of key requests can contain:
.BR ecdsa\-p256 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p384 ,
or
.BI rsa\- bits
for RSA keys of at least
.I bits
bits, from 2048 to 8192.
.TP
.BI \-\-name\-type " type"
A kind of name certificates can carry:
.BR dns ,
.B email
or
.BR upn .
.TP
.BI \-\-common\-name " source"
Where the common name comes from:
.BR none ,
.BR first\-name ,
the default, or
.BR owner\-display\-name .
.TP
.BI \-\-issuance " issuance"
.BR immediate ,
the default, issues what policy permits at once;
.B approval
holds every request for an officer.
.TP
.BI \-\-enroll\-security " sddl"
Who can use the template: Enroll (0x1) lets a principal submit requests under it.
Enrollment alone grants nothing; every name requested must also be permitted.
.SH NAMES
A name is a host name, such as
.BR radius.lab.home ,
or a principal name,
.IR user @ domain ,
such as
.BR jack@home ,
as email and UPN certificates carry.
.BI @ domain
names every principal at
.IR domain ,
whose descriptor they inherit.
.B .
and
.B @
name the roots of the two trees.
Names compare without regard to case.
A wildcard name has no entry of its own: Wildcard on
.B lab.home
permits
.BR *.lab.home .
.PP
A descriptor given in SDDL can use SID aliases, such as
.B BA
for Administrators and
.B AU
for Authenticated Users.
.B pki
warns when a name's own descriptor lets a broad group, such as Authenticated Users or Users, request it without approval, and when such a group is made an owner: every member could then obtain certificates for the name.
A tree's root inherits nothing, so its descriptor is written as given;
.B pki
warns when one leaves Administrators unable to request the names in the tree, or owners unable to request their own.
Prefer Request with approval (0x2) for groups.
.SH PASSPHRASES
A key in a sealed file signs only with its passphrase, and
.B pki
asks for it on the terminal, without echoing it, whenever a command needs it.
A new passphrase is asked for twice and must have at least 15 characters.
.PP
A script gives a passphrase in a file instead, as the file's first line.
Each command names its files by what they unlock:
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase\-file
The new key file's, for
.BR "ca create" ;
the CA key's, for
.BR "ca sign" ;
the sealed key file's, for
.BR "ca import" ;
the key's current one, for
.BR "ca export\-key" ;
the PKCS #12 file's, and a held key's, for
.BR enroll .
.TP
.B \-\-issuer\-passphrase\-file
The issuing CA key's, for
.B ca create
with
.BR \-\-issuer .
.TP
.B \-\-new\-passphrase\-file
The new key file's, for
.BR "ca import" ;
the exported file's, for
.BR "ca export\-key" .
.PP
.B pki
never takes a passphrase from its arguments or the environment, where other processes can read it.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
each command prints one JSON object on standard output.
A CA has
.BR id ,
.BR name ,
.B state
.RB ( issuing
or
.BR awaiting\-certificate ),
.BR key ,
.BR key_available ,
.B templates
(each with
.B id
and
.BR name ),
and
.BR certificate :
its
.BR subject ,
.BR issuer ,
.BR serial ,
.BR not_before ,
.B not_after
and
.BR sha256 ,
or null.
.B ca list
prints
.BR authorities ;
.B ca show
prints one CA, and
.BR "ca create" ,
.BR "ca install" ,
.B ca import
and
.B ca set
print it under
.BR created ,
.BR installed ,
.B imported
or
.BR changed .
.B ca sign
prints the
.B certificate
in PEM.
.PP
A request has
.BR id ,
.B status
.RB ( pending ,
.B issued
or
.BR denied ),
.B template
and
.BR template_name ,
.BR names ,
.BR requester ,
.BR submitted ,
.BR reasons ,
.B decided
and
.BR decider .
.B request list
prints
.BR requests ,
and
.B request show
prints one.
.B request submit
and
.B request approve
print the
.BR request 's
number and
.BR status ,
with the
.B certificate
in PEM when it was issued, or the
.B reasons
it is held.
.PP
A certificate has
.B serial
(in hex),
.BR request ,
.B status
.RB ( valid ,
.B expired
or
.BR revoked ),
.BR subject ,
.BR not_before ,
.BR not_after ,
.BR owner ,
.B sha256
and
.BR revocation :
its
.BR revoked ,
.B reason
and
.BR revoker ,
or null.
.B cert list
prints
.BR certificates ;
.B cert show
and
.B cert get
print one, with
.B cert get
adding the
.B certificate
in PEM.
.B enroll
prints the
.BR request 's
number,
.BR status ,
and the file
.BR written ,
with the certificate's
.B subject
and
.BR not_after ,
or, when held, the
.B key_file
the key is sealed in.
.PP
A template has
.BR id ,
.BR name ,
.BR purposes ,
.BR key_usages ,
.BR lifetime_days ,
.BR renewal_window_days ,
.BR key_algorithms ,
.BR name_types ,
.BR common_name ,
.BR issuance ,
.B enroll_security
(SDDL) and
.B used_by
(the CAs that issue from it).
.B template list
prints
.BR templates ;
.B template show
prints one, and
.B template create
and
.B template set
print it under
.B created
or
.BR changed .
.PP
A name has
.BR name ,
.B tree
.RB ( Dns
or
.BR Principals ),
.BR exists ,
.BR owner ,
.B security
(SDDL) and
.BR inherited .
.B name list
prints
.BR names ;
.B name show
prints one, and
.B name add
and the
.B name set
commands print it under
.B added
or
.BR changed .
.PP
Times are in RFC 3339, in UTC, and people are named by their SIDs.
A file written is named by
.B written
or, for
.BR \-\-offline ,
.BR request_file .
.PP
An error is printed on standard error as
.B error
with a
.B code
.RB ( failed ,
.BR invalid\-argument ,
.BR permission\-denied ,
.B not\-found
or
.BR already\-exists )
and a
.BR message .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.BR setup ,
the
.B template
commands and the
.B name
commands read and write
.B Machine\eSoftware\ePki
directly, each change in one Registry transaction.
The
.B ca
commands read it only to find templates by name.
Each value is documented in
.BR regman (1);
run
.BR "regman Machine\eSoftware\ePki" .
.SH SECURITY
.B pki
grants nothing itself.
.B pkid
decides each
.BR ca ,
.BR request ,
.B cert
and
.B enroll
command by your own access token, against the CA's descriptor, the template's and the names'; see
.BR pkid (8).
The commands that write the Registry need write access to the keys under
.BR Machine\eSoftware\ePki ,
which Administrators have by default.
Whoever can write them decides which certificates are issued and to whom.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as
.B pkid
not running, a passphrase that is wrong or too short, a certificate that does not fit its CA, or a request the CA refused.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown command or option, a missing argument, a value that is not valid, such as a template
.B pkid
would refuse, or a CA name more than one CA has.
.TP
.B 3
Access denied: you cannot do this to the CA, change the configuration, or run
.BR setup ,
or the request you would approve is your own.
.TP
.B 4
Not found: no CA, template, name, request or certificate is the one given, or a file does not exist.
.TP
.B 5
Already exists: an output file exists, the CA already has its certificate, or a template or name of that name exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pki setup
Prepare a new machine for
.BR pkid .
.TP
.B pki ca create """Lab Root""" \-\-root \-\-key\-file /media/usb/root.key
Create a root CA whose key is sealed on removable media.
.TP
.B pki ca create """Lab Issuing""" \-\-issuer """Lab Root""" \-\-template Server \-\-template Device
Create a CA that issues unattended from
.BR pkid 's
store, signed by the root, which asks for the root key's passphrase.
.TP
.B pki ca show """Lab Root""" \-\-out lab\-root.pem
Write the root's certificate, to give to the machines that are to trust it.
.TP
.B pki ca export\-key """Lab Issuing""" issuing.key
Back up an exportable CA key.
.TP
.B pki request submit """Lab Issuing""" Server www.csr \-\-out www.pem
Ask for a server certificate.
.TP
.B pki request list """Lab Issuing""" \-\-status pending
List the requests waiting for an officer.
.TP
.B pki enroll """Lab Issuing""" Device \-\-upn alice@home \-\-out alice.p12
Make a certificate for Alice's phone to sign in to Wi-Fi with, and its key, in one file.
.TP
.B pki template create Web \-\-from Server \-\-issuance approval
Make a server template whose every request waits for an officer.
.TP
.B pki name set\-security lab.home """D:(A;CI;0x2;;;AU)"""
Let anyone signed in ask for certificates for names under
.BR lab.home ,
each held for approval.
.TP
.B pki name set\-owner radius.lab.home S\-1\-5\-21\-1\-2\-3\-1105
Let the RADIUS server's principal request its own name without approval, through the roots' PRINCIPAL SELF grant.
.TP
.B pki cert revoke """Lab Issuing""" 4f:1a:09 \-\-reason key\-compromise
Revoke a certificate whose key was exposed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR regman (1),
.BR pkid (8)
.PP
